# Swiftline Portal- scheduled maintenance jobs
#
# Install as /etc/cron.d/swiftline-jobs (root-owned, mode 0644, and it MUST end
# with a newline or cron silently ignores the last entry).
#
# Cadences come from each script's own header comment. Every job is idempotent
# and self-healing: a missed run is picked up by the next one, so a reboot or a
# deploy in the middle of the schedule needs no manual catch-up.
#
# Times are IST because that is the day boundary the credit module bills on-
# see the Asia/Kolkata handling in dailyTopUpLimit.service.ts. CRON_TZ makes the
# entries below mean what they say regardless of the server clock, which on EC2
# is normally UTC.

CRON_TZ=Asia/Kolkata
SHELL=/bin/bash
MAILTO=""

# Release booking holds whose TTL passed without the booking completing.
# Left unrun, an abandoned booking keeps consuming the client's credit forever.
*/5 * * * *   ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:credit:expire-reservations

# Retry queued email that the post-enqueue drain did not manage to send, and
# reclaim rows stranded in SENDING by a crash. First-attempt sends already happen
# in-process; this is what walks the 1m/5m/30m/2h/12h backoff ladder behind them.
*/5 * * * *   ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:email:drain

# Delete expired unpaid public booking drafts and their sensitive KYC uploads.
# Paid and payment-review records are deliberately outside this sweep.
17 * * * *    ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:public-bookings:purge-expired

# Alert admin and operations when a support ticket misses its first-response
# deadline. Nothing else can raise this: every other ticket notification hangs
# off somebody acting, and a breach happens precisely because nobody did- so
# without a scheduled pass the one event that most needs attention is the only
# one that goes unannounced. Escalation is stamped on the ticket, so a breach is
# announced once however often this runs, and a ticket answered late is never
# alerted on at all.
*/20 * * * *  ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:tickets:sla-escalation

# Evaluate active flight delays, customs deadlines, manifest gaps, and
# connection risks even when nobody has opened the flight detail page. Each
# exception and its alert is deduplicated by a stable key.
*/10 * * * *  ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:flight:exceptions

# Mark due-passed statements overdue, expire credit facilities past their
# validity window, and send utilisation warnings.
# Note: overdue *blocking* is computed live at booking time and does not depend
# on this job. What depends on it is the reported status, facility expiry, and
# the warnings clients receive.
7 * * * *     ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:credit:mark-overdue

# Issue statements for completed billing periods. This is the job that turns a
# period of shipments into something a client can be asked to pay.
0 2 * * *     ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:credit:close-billing

# Warn clients whose appeal window is closing (7/3/1 days) and flag claims past
# their review target. Nothing else triggers these: they are due to time passing.
30 2 * * *    ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:claims:sweep-deadlines

# Read-only. Re-adds every credit account against its ledger and notifies admins
# on drift. Corrects nothing by design; it is the smoke detector for the money.
0 3 * * *     ubuntu  /var/www/Swiftline-portal/backend/deploy/jobs/run-job.sh job:credit:reconcile

# Deliberately NOT scheduled: purging claim evidence past its eight-year
# retention is destructive and needs `--apply` to do anything. Nothing in the
# system can be eight years old yet. Run it as a reported dry run first:
#
#   npm run job:claims:purge-expired
#
# and only schedule it once the output has been reviewed and the retention
# policy signed off.
